# The TNG add-on's own viewer - the mission that ships WITH the mod source.
#
#   python -m cosmos_dev.mission_runner Cosmos-TNG-Mod --gui
#   open http://localhost:8765/server
#
# WHY THIS LIVES AT THE REPO ROOT. A mod repo is otherwise just an addon: nothing in it can
# be run, so having the source in hand gives you nothing to look at. Putting a mission
# beside the addon folders - the shape LegendaryMissions and OpenUniverse already use -
# means the repo tests itself. It needs ONLY sbs_utils and this mod: no LegendaryMissions,
# which is the part that makes the pattern portable to any other mod.
#
# It has to be the ROOT, not a subfolder. Three things are one-level-only: `pinned_packs`
# globs missions/*/story.json and `prune_media` DELETES any unpacked pack nothing pins (so a
# nested mission would have the 405MB media pack pruned out from under it), mission
# discovery is one level, and a mission inside tng_races/ would be zipped INTO the mastlib
# and compiled into every consuming mission.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT TEST. MAST prefers source over a declared lib, so this loads
# tng_races/ from SOURCE - a fast edit-and-run loop, but it cannot catch "forgot to rebuild
# the lib". That is TNG_TestRange's job: a sibling folder with no tng_races/ of its own, so
# it loads the BUILT mastlib inside a full LegendaryMissions install. Two missions, two
# different questions. Keep both.

# No client-connect dialog. This is a VIEWER - nobody is joining a bridge, so the server's
# "a client connected" prompt is pure nag here, and it interrupts the operator's own screen.
#
# THE ARGUMENT IS 0, AND THAT IS BACKWARDS FROM THE NAME - CONFIRMED IN THE ENGINE. The flag
# is the state of the DIALOG, not of the suppression: 0 = dialog off, 1 = dialog on, despite
# the function being called suppress_*. Do not 'correct' this to 1; it reads like a bug and
# is not one.
sbs.suppress_client_connect_dialog(0)

sim_create()
await delay_app(0.1)
sim_resume()


# THE PICKER LIVES IN sbs_utils NOW, so this mission is two @map labels and three lines.
#
# It used to be a hand-rolled menu of buttons, because @map was unusable without
# LegendaryMissions: LM's server console draws the selection screen, so with sbs_utils alone
# the server page came up empty with no way in. gui_map_picker() is that screen, generalized
# into the library - a carousel of the story's @map labels plus each map's Properties panel -
# and map_start() is the launch sequence that used to be duplicated (and drifted) between
# LM's console and the headless runner.
#
# main FALLS THROUGH into this label, which is what keeps main - and therefore page.gui_task -
# alive. Do not move it to an imported file: main only falls through into a label in its OWN
# file, and a dead gui_task makes every gui_task_jump a silent no-op.
=== tngview_picker
---pick
    chosen = await gui_map_picker()
    map_start(chosen)
---running
    # PARK - do NOT ->END here, and this is not defensive padding.
    #
    # main IS page.gui_task. The started map's body runs on its own scheduled task and
    # calls gui_task_jump to put its page on the gui task, and gui_task_jump silently
    # DISCARDS a jump when that task has finished (it checks `task is not None` but never
    # `task.done`, and tick() returns early on a done task before it looks at
    # pending_jump). Ending main here therefore killed the gui task a moment before the
    # map tried to use it: no error, no page, blank screen.
    await delay_sim(1)
    jump running


@map/tng_faces "TNG Faces - browse every face the mod ships"
    " Every face in the add-on's registry, by species. The registry is the same one
    " random_face() reads, so this cannot drift from the shipped sheets.
    gui_task_jump("tng_faces_page")
    ->END


@map/tng_ships "TNG Ships - browse every hull the mod declares"
    " Every declared hull, grouped by faction, with its stats and its 3D model. The verify
    " mission proves the DATA arrived; this is what shows the ART actually renders.
    gui_task_jump("tng_ships_page")
    ->END


# IMPORTS LAST. `import` INLINES the file where it appears, so imports placed above the
# top-level code would push it behind a run of `===` labels - it would stop being main's
# code and become the tail of whichever label was inlined last.
import maps/tng_faces.mast
import maps/tng_ship_info.py
import maps/tng_ships.mast
